Maybe the attack scenario would be something like:

1) the consumer server uses session-based sessions for accessing JWK sets on another server (`https://keyserver.internal/keys.jwks`);
2) the attacker would forge a JWK with a `jku` URI pointing to `https://keyserver.internal/keys.jwks`;
3) the consumer server would fetch the JWKs using the cookies (or some other kind of credentials).

This way the attacker might trick the consumer server into using some private/internal key material for some unintended usage. But I'm not sure what the impact would be.

This is quite far-fetched.
